WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.715
CURVE 35.

2
00:00:02.715 --> 00:00:04.249
Build your own film camera.

3
00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:09.425
The camera is 9 pieces you put together: 8 printed parts and one lens.

4
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:13.337
It goes together with no tools, no glue and no screws.

5
00:00:13.887 --> 00:00:17.012
You can print it free, from the files, or buy the kit.

6
00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:22.988
CURVE 35 is for ages 14 and up; younger builders can build it with an adult.

7
00:00:23.538 --> 00:00:28.318
This film walks through every step: 10 to build it, 6 to load the film,

8
00:00:28.318 --> 00:00:33.335
4 for every picture, 2 to take the film out, and 3 for the pinhole experiment.

9
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.002
Ten steps.

10
00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.507
Steps 1 to 4 make the dark box.

11
00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:42.567
Steps 5 to 10 put the orange front plate on it, with the shutter,

12
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:43.975
the wheel and the lens.

13
00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:45.765
Build step one.

14
00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:48.750
Take the spool, the part with the red winder.

15
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.820
Lower it, winder up, into the round chamber on the right, seen from behind.

16
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.770
Its plain end goes into the slot in the floor,

17
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.572
and the winder's neck into the slot in the roof.

18
00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:03.502
Check it: the red winder sits on top of the camera and turns.

19
00:01:03.502 --> 00:01:07.327
The spool, its axle and its winder are one piece,

20
00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:10.008
because this part takes the most turning force.

21
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:12.633
Step two.

22
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:15.478
Fit the green winder into the slot on the left.

23
00:01:15.478 --> 00:01:16.743
Give it a turn.

24
00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.658
It turns, and it only comes out backwards.

25
00:01:19.658 --> 00:01:22.553
This winder will turn the film's own spool:

26
00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:25.683
its forked end fits over a bar inside the film.

27
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:29.653
So the green winder winds each picture back into the film's shell,

28
00:01:29.653 --> 00:01:32.248
and the red winder is where the film starts.

29
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:34.842
Step three.

30
00:01:34.842 --> 00:01:37.207
Lay the back down, inside up.

31
00:01:37.207 --> 00:01:40.962
Hold the blue frame wheel with its dial just past the edge.

32
00:01:40.962 --> 00:01:46.272
Lay its neck in the fork, then slide it toward the long round post until its teeth

33
00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.891
touch the end of the post.

34
00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:51.266
The wheel turns freely, and it does not lift out.

35
00:01:51.266 --> 00:01:55.891
If it does lift out, it was not all the way home: move it toward the edge,

36
00:01:55.891 --> 00:01:58.256
press it down into the fork, and move it back.

37
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:00.694
Step four.

38
00:02:00.694 --> 00:02:03.117
Press the back on until both ends click.

39
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:04.207
Two clicks.

40
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:06.377
Now try to pull it off: it stays.

41
00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:08.819
Pinch the two pads and it lets go.

42
00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:13.304
A nub on a springy finger at each end comes up behind a bar on the body,

43
00:02:13.304 --> 00:02:17.514
and a ridge all round the back sits in a groove to keep the light out.

44
00:02:17.514 --> 00:02:19.564
This is the dark box.

45
00:02:19.564 --> 00:02:21.478
Everything else goes on its front.

46
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.170
Step five, and the front plate.

47
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.810
Lay it face down.

48
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.135
Drop the shutter onto the round peg near the top edge, with the rocker,

49
00:02:31.135 --> 00:02:34.105
the bar across its top, hanging over the edge.

50
00:02:34.105 --> 00:02:38.815
The rocker sits just outside the edge; the shutter swings and clicks.

51
00:02:38.815 --> 00:02:41.880
If it will not lie flat, it is upside down.

52
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.147
The peg is the shutter's pivot, and the shutter's own rim is its spring.

53
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:48.832
Step six.

54
00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:51.007
Pick the plate up and hold it flat.

55
00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:56.027
Drop the aperture wheel onto the other peg, handle down through the curved slot.

56
00:02:56.027 --> 00:03:00.295
The handle sticks out of the front, and the wheel clicks into three places.

57
00:03:00.295 --> 00:03:04.850
If the wheel will not lie flat, its handle is not in the slot yet.

58
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.844
The wheel carries three holes of different sizes, and its rim is a spring too.

59
00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:12.497
Step seven.

60
00:03:12.497 --> 00:03:13.992
Keep the plate flat.

61
00:03:13.992 --> 00:03:19.092
Lower the camera onto it, front down, a little high and a little to one side,

62
00:03:19.092 --> 00:03:22.282
until its four pockets drop over the plate's four feet.

63
00:03:22.282 --> 00:03:25.885
The camera lies flat on the plate and nothing rocks.

64
00:03:25.885 --> 00:03:31.350
If it rocks, move it about a few millimetres: the feet drop in at one place only.

65
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.424
Each pocket is open at one corner: that is the way in, and the only way out.

66
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:38.993
Step eight.

67
00:03:38.993 --> 00:03:41.963
Pinch camera and plate together and turn them over.

68
00:03:41.963 --> 00:03:45.073
Slide the plate sideways until it stops.

69
00:03:45.073 --> 00:03:49.723
The point on the plate's top edge now lines up with the middle line on top of the

70
00:03:49.723 --> 00:03:50.375
camera.

71
00:03:50.375 --> 00:03:55.530
If it will not slide, it is not flat on the camera, so go back one step.

72
00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.815
Now the lips on the feet are under the camera's skin,

73
00:03:58.815 --> 00:04:01.123
and the plate can no longer be lifted off.

74
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:03.743
Step nine.

75
00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.138
Slide the plate down until it clicks.

76
00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:10.128
One click, and its top edge is level with the top of the camera.

77
00:04:10.128 --> 00:04:11.248
No click?

78
00:04:11.248 --> 00:04:14.274
Press the plate flat against the body while you slide.

79
00:04:14.274 --> 00:04:18.194
The hooks are home and a springy tongue has dropped behind a bump.

80
00:04:18.194 --> 00:04:23.269
To come off, the plate has to go up past the click, then sideways, then away.

81
00:04:23.269 --> 00:04:25.228
A knock cannot do all three.

82
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.758
Step ten, the last one.

83
00:04:28.758 --> 00:04:34.598
Press the lens into the round pocket on the front, bulge outward, until it stops.

84
00:04:34.598 --> 00:04:37.953
It sits level and stays in when you shake the camera.

85
00:04:37.953 --> 00:04:40.948
Three small ribs in the pocket hold the lens;

86
00:04:40.948 --> 00:04:43.553
nothing else in the camera is held this way.

87
00:04:43.553 --> 00:04:47.273
If the lens is loose, or will not go in, tell whoever made the kit.

88
00:04:47.273 --> 00:04:49.078
Press the rocker: click.

89
00:04:49.078 --> 00:04:51.198
Slide the handle: three clicks.

90
00:04:51.198 --> 00:04:51.811
Done.

91
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:54.703
Loading the film.

92
00:04:54.703 --> 00:04:58.338
Find some shade first: never do this in bright sun.

93
00:04:58.338 --> 00:05:02.208
Pinch the two pads, top and bottom, and lift the back off.

94
00:05:02.208 --> 00:05:07.053
The blue frame wheel comes away with the back, so lay the back down somewhere safe,

95
00:05:07.053 --> 00:05:08.161
inside up.

96
00:05:08.161 --> 00:05:12.441
From here until the back is closed again, keep the film out of the sun.

97
00:05:12.441 --> 00:05:15.453
Next, the green winder is fitted to the film.

98
00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:19.457
Take the green winder out of the camera.

99
00:05:19.457 --> 00:05:22.607
Fit its fork over the bar in the open end of the film.

100
00:05:22.607 --> 00:05:26.820
Now turn the winder: the spool inside the film turns with it.

101
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.815
Wrong end?

102
00:05:27.815 --> 00:05:30.600
The other end of the film has a peg sticking out.

103
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.815
The fork straddles a bar inside the film's spool,

104
00:05:33.815 --> 00:05:37.155
so from now on this winder turns the film's own spool.

105
00:05:38.867 --> 00:05:42.392
Lower the film and the winder in together, on the left,

106
00:05:42.392 --> 00:05:45.442
with the film's lip pointing to the middle of the camera.

107
00:05:45.442 --> 00:05:47.735
Keep them together as they go in.

108
00:05:47.735 --> 00:05:53.150
The film sits flat in its chamber, and the green winder sits in its slot on top,

109
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.025
just as it did before.

110
00:05:55.025 --> 00:05:57.290
If the film will not sit flat,

111
00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.441
lift it out and try again with the lip facing the middle.

112
00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:05.987
Turn the red winder until the slot in its spool faces you.

113
00:06:05.987 --> 00:06:09.427
Pull the film across and tuck its tip into the slot.

114
00:06:09.427 --> 00:06:11.837
Give the red winder one turn.

115
00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:15.821
The film is caught, and it lies over the two curved rails.

116
00:06:15.821 --> 00:06:19.496
If the tip slips out, tuck it deeper and turn again.

117
00:06:19.496 --> 00:06:22.321
The film lies on a curve around the lens:

118
00:06:22.321 --> 00:06:26.437
that curve is why one simple lens can be sharp from corner to corner.

119
00:06:28.167 --> 00:06:29.142
Close the back.

120
00:06:29.142 --> 00:06:32.212
Press until you hear two clicks, one at each end.

121
00:06:32.212 --> 00:06:34.757
Keep it shut until the film is out again.

122
00:06:34.757 --> 00:06:38.292
The frame wheel's teeth press on the film's top edge.

123
00:06:38.292 --> 00:06:41.517
If they are not in the film's holes yet, do not worry:

124
00:06:41.517 --> 00:06:44.062
the first turn of the winder drops them in.

125
00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:46.631
From now on, the film is in the dark.

126
00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:52.078
Turn the red winder the way the picture shows until it stops:

127
00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:58.053
about 25 turns for a film of 24 pictures, about 33 for 36.

128
00:06:58.053 --> 00:07:03.885
While you turn, the arrow on the blue dial goes round and round: the film is moving.

129
00:07:03.885 --> 00:07:08.880
If the arrow does not move, open the back in the shade and tuck the film in again.

130
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.400
When the winder stops, stop.

131
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.485
Every picture you take will be wound back into the shell,

132
00:07:14.485 --> 00:07:16.048
where light cannot reach it.

133
00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:21.853
Four steps for every picture: choose a dot, aim, press, wind.

134
00:07:22.403 --> 00:07:24.368
Taking a picture, step one.

135
00:07:24.368 --> 00:07:26.898
Slide the handle on the front to a dot.

136
00:07:26.898 --> 00:07:29.958
The chart shows which one, for your film and the light.

137
00:07:29.958 --> 00:07:31.565
It clicks at each dot.

138
00:07:31.565 --> 00:07:34.495
The dot shows the size of the hole behind it,

139
00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:38.330
and each dot lets in about twice the light of the one before.

140
00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:43.628
When even the largest dot is too small for a snap, take a time picture instead.

141
00:07:44.178 --> 00:07:45.978
Lesson 4, Aperture.

142
00:07:45.978 --> 00:07:48.848
The size of the hole sets how much light gets in;

143
00:07:48.848 --> 00:07:51.453
each f-number step halves or doubles it.

144
00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:54.687
Step two: aim.

145
00:07:54.687 --> 00:07:56.782
Look along the middle line on top,

146
00:07:56.782 --> 00:08:00.022
from the edge of the pad at the back to the point at the front.

147
00:08:00.022 --> 00:08:02.961
The picture fits between the two outer lines.

148
00:08:02.961 --> 00:08:06.881
Stand at least two big steps away from what you are photographing.

149
00:08:06.881 --> 00:08:11.736
Nearer than that and the picture is not sharp, because the focus is fixed.

150
00:08:11.736 --> 00:08:14.959
Put what matters in the middle: it is sharpest there.

151
00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:18.492
Step three.

152
00:08:18.492 --> 00:08:19.138
Hold still.

153
00:08:19.138 --> 00:08:23.267
Press the raised side of the rocker flat, in one quick press.

154
00:08:23.267 --> 00:08:25.007
One click.

155
00:08:25.007 --> 00:08:27.194
Now the other side is up: that is the side to press next time.

156
00:08:27.194 --> 00:08:31.884
If the rocker stops level, the shutter is open, so press it on down.

157
00:08:31.884 --> 00:08:35.599
A window in the shutter passes the hole as it swings:

158
00:08:35.599 --> 00:08:39.504
a quick press lets light in for about a three-hundredth of a second.

159
00:08:40.054 --> 00:08:41.879
Lesson 5, The shutter.

160
00:08:41.879 --> 00:08:44.694
A shutter lets light in for a set instant;

161
00:08:44.694 --> 00:08:48.079
this one is a plastic blade whose own rim is its spring.

162
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.645
Step four: wind on.

163
00:08:51.645 --> 00:08:57.570
Turn the green winder the way the picture shows until the arrow on the blue dial has

164
00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:58.277
gone once round.

165
00:08:58.277 --> 00:08:59.719
Leave the red winder alone.

166
00:08:59.719 --> 00:09:02.799
The arrow points at its mark on the body again.

167
00:09:02.799 --> 00:09:08.164
If the winder resists, stop and let go of the red winder: do not force it.

168
00:09:08.164 --> 00:09:11.379
One turn of the dial is one picture's length of film.

169
00:09:11.929 --> 00:09:14.089
Lesson 6, Winding on.

170
00:09:14.089 --> 00:09:16.824
The film's own holes turn the frame wheel,

171
00:09:16.824 --> 00:09:19.791
so one turn of the dial is exactly one picture.

172
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:22.360
Two steps.

173
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.848
Then the film goes to a lab to be developed.

174
00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:26.833
The film is finished.

175
00:09:26.833 --> 00:09:32.608
After the last picture, keep turning the green winder until the arrow on the dial

176
00:09:32.608 --> 00:09:33.217
stops moving.

177
00:09:33.217 --> 00:09:34.873
Then give it three more turns.

178
00:09:34.873 --> 00:09:39.703
The arrow no longer moves: all the film is back in its shell, in the dark.

179
00:09:39.703 --> 00:09:42.503
Only now is it safe to open the back.

180
00:09:42.503 --> 00:09:47.831
If the arrow is still moving, keep winding; never open the back before it stops.

181
00:09:49.533 --> 00:09:50.593
Open the back.

182
00:09:50.593 --> 00:09:54.203
Lift the film out with its winder, and take the winder off.

183
00:09:54.203 --> 00:09:58.221
The film's tip is inside the shell, or just peeping out.

184
00:09:58.221 --> 00:10:02.816
Send the film off with your developing card, or take it to any photo lab:

185
00:10:02.816 --> 00:10:06.816
any lab that develops 35 millimetre film can do it.

186
00:10:06.816 --> 00:10:09.731
Ask for scans, and keep the negatives.

187
00:10:09.731 --> 00:10:11.182
They are the originals.

188
00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.385
The pinhole experiment.

189
00:10:14.385 --> 00:10:17.855
Take the pinhole disc from the bag, or make one.

190
00:10:17.855 --> 00:10:23.157
To make one, ask an adult to cut a square about 10 mm across from a clean drinks can

191
00:10:23.157 --> 00:10:26.626
with kitchen scissors, and to snip off its corners.

192
00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:29.351
Lay it on card, press a pin into the middle,

193
00:10:29.351 --> 00:10:31.961
then push just until the tip comes through.

194
00:10:31.961 --> 00:10:36.093
Hold it up to a lamp: you should see one tiny round point of light.

195
00:10:36.643 --> 00:10:38.503
Lesson 1, The pinhole.

196
00:10:38.503 --> 00:10:40.783
Light travels in straight lines,

197
00:10:40.783 --> 00:10:45.838
so a small hole lets through from each point of a scene only the rays lined up with

198
00:10:45.838 --> 00:10:47.415
it: an upside-down picture.

199
00:10:49.133 --> 00:10:50.508
Leave the lens out.

200
00:10:50.508 --> 00:10:54.423
Lay the disc flat in the round pocket, hole in the middle,

201
00:10:54.423 --> 00:10:58.118
and press the pinhole ring in on top of it until it stops.

202
00:10:58.118 --> 00:11:01.970
The disc covers the hole in the floor of the pocket all round.

203
00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.090
The ring stands a little proud and does not wobble.

204
00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:10.215
Same plate, same pocket: with the disc and ring it is a pinhole camera.

205
00:11:10.215 --> 00:11:13.230
To change back, lift the ring out with a fingernail.

206
00:11:14.933 --> 00:11:16.343
Set the large dot.

207
00:11:16.343 --> 00:11:18.728
Stand the camera on something steady.

208
00:11:18.728 --> 00:11:23.418
Hold the rocker level to open it, count, then press it down, and wind on.

209
00:11:23.418 --> 00:11:27.750
With ISO 400 film: in sun, open and shut.

210
00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.395
Cloud, count 1.

211
00:11:29.395 --> 00:11:31.135
Shade, count 3.

212
00:11:31.135 --> 00:11:33.850
Indoors by a window, count 40.

213
00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:38.505
The pinhole lets in about 150 times less light than the large dot,

214
00:11:38.505 --> 00:11:41.478
so a pinhole picture is counted, not snapped.

215
00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.755
That is the whole camera.

216
00:11:44.755 --> 00:11:51.762
The 9 lessons explain how it works, one idea each, at curve35.com/lessons.

217
00:11:52.312 --> 00:11:57.682
The final roll: 24 frames that use every lesson, and a rubric to grade it.

218
00:11:57.682 --> 00:12:01.250
It is at curve35.com/final.

219
00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:07.116
If a step will not go, the help page is at curve35.com/help.

